# === Keyboard ===

# === input_lock — kb_buf SMP/ISR serialization (1.46.x SMP arc, sub-bite 5) ===
# kb_buf is an SPSC-ish ring with MULTIPLE producers advancing kb_head — kb_isr
# (the IRQ1 PS/2 ISR, hand-asm below) AND hid_kb_push (the xHCI USB path, hid.cyr)
# — plus one consumer advancing kb_tail (kb_read_scancode). The `(idx+1)&0xFF`
# index update is a non-atomic load-modify-store, so a producer interleaving another
# producer/consumer (an IRQ1 mid-RMW same-CPU, or a foreign CPU once APs run real
# procs at STEP-2) loses or corrupts a keystroke. input_lock serializes the kb_buf
# critical sections: **cli FIRST** (mask the same-CPU IRQ1 producer so a plain
# spinlock can never self-deadlock against kb_isr) **THEN the xchg spinlock** (the
# cross-CPU guard). Structured byte-identically to pmm_spin_lock — NO parameter, so
# the hardcoded [rbp-N] frame offsets are exact (a parameterized spin_lock_at spills
# the param and shifts them — the SMP review's confirmed-broken finding).
#
# kb_isr itself is LEFT UNMODIFIED: it runs at IF=0 (ISR), so any C-path holding
# input_lock with cli excludes it on the same CPU, and IRQ1 is PIC/IOAPIC-routed to
# the BSP while the kb_buf consumers (agnsh read loop) run BSP-pinned (foreground,
# via idle_owner) — so kb_isr never contends cross-CPU under the current single-IRQ1
# routing. (If IRQ1 is ever redirected to an AP, kb_isr must take input_lock too —
# that means emitting the xchg inline in its hand-asm stub.) The kb_has_key compare
# stays unlocked: a single consumer owns kb_tail, producers only grow kb_head, so a
# stale read is benign (the key is seen on the next poll).
var input_lock = 0;
fn input_spin_lock() {
    var old = 1;
    while (old == 1) {
        var addr = &input_lock;
        asm {
            0x48; 0x8B; 0x45; 0xF0;      # mov rax, [rbp-0x10] (addr)
            0x48; 0xC7; 0xC1; 0x01; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00;  # mov rcx, 1
            0x48; 0x87; 0x08;              # xchg [rax], rcx
            0x48; 0x89; 0x4D; 0xF8;       # mov [rbp-0x08], rcx (old)
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
fn input_spin_unlock() {
    var addr = &input_lock;
    asm {
        0x48; 0x8B; 0x45; 0xF8;      # mov rax, [rbp-0x08] (addr)
        0x48; 0x31; 0xC9;              # xor rcx, rcx
        0x48; 0x87; 0x08;              # xchg [rax], rcx
    }
    return 0;
}
# IRQ-save/restore (sole-local frame shape, like pmm_spin_unlock's addr). kbd_irq_save
# captures RFLAGS THEN cli's; kbd_irq_restore re-enables ONLY if IF (bit 9) was set —
# so an input_lock critical section is correct even from a future IF=0 caller (e.g.
# hid_poll serviced from the timer ISR), not just the current IF=1 read-loop callers.
fn kbd_irq_save() {
    var f = 0;
    asm {
        0x9C;                          # pushfq
        0x58;                          # pop rax
        0x48; 0x89; 0x45; 0xF8;        # mov [rbp-0x08], rax  (f = RFLAGS)
        0xFA;                          # cli
    }
    return f;
}
fn kbd_irq_restore(f) {
    if ((f & 0x200) != 0) {
        asm { 0xFB; }                  # sti (IF was set on entry)
    }
    return 0;
}

# ⛔⛔ THE PS/2 ISR IS DELETED (2026-08-08, operator ruling: no i8042 in agnos, in any capacity).
#
# `kb_isr_build()` lived here and hand-assembled 83 bytes of machine code whose middle instruction was
# `in al, 0x60` — the i8042 data port. It was installed on IDT vector 33 and EOI'd the legacy 8259.
#
# ⚠ IT WAS DEAD ON THE TARGET AND ALIVE IN THE EMULATOR, WHICH IS THE WORST COMBINATION. archaemenid has
# no PS/2 port and its firmware does not emulate PS/2 over xHCI after ExitBootServices (CHANGELOG 1.30.9,
# iron attempt 68) — and agnos itself disarms the SMM route during xHCI init, since
# `xhci_usblegsup_claim()` clears the USBLEGCTLSTS SMI enables including "SMI on USB IRQ". But QEMU's q35
# always has a real i8042, so in emulation this path DID deliver keys — masking the xHCI HID path
# completely. A mutation test proved it: the xHCI endpoint dispatch was forced to always miss and the
# keyboard still typed in QEMU. Every keyboard gate this project ran in emulation was measuring the path
# the hardware does not have.
#
# ⇒ Keys now arrive one way on both substrates: xHCI HID -> `hid_poll` -> `hid_kb_push` -> `kb_buf`.
# ⚠ `kb_buf`, `kb_head`, the `input_lock` family and `scancode_to_ascii`'s **Set-1 tables** all SURVIVE
# and are not PS/2: Set-1 is the USB path's wire format — `hid_translate.cyr` maps HID usages to Set-1
# and `hid_kb_push` writes them into this same ring. Deleting them would delete the keyboard.

fn kb_has_key() {
    # ⭐ ONE PRODUCER: `hid_poll()` drains the xHCI USB-HID event ring and `hid_kb_push` appends Set-1
    # scancodes to kb_buf. That is the whole story on both substrates as of 2026-08-08.
    # ⛔ There used to be a second producer — `kb_isr`, the PS/2 IRQ1 handler reading port 0x60 — and this
    # comment claimed IT was "the producer that delivers on archaemenid". That was false: the box has no
    # PS/2 port, and agnos itself disarms the firmware's SMM legacy route during xHCI init
    # (`xhci_usblegsup_claim` clears the USBLEGCTLSTS SMI enables). It IS real in QEMU's q35, which is how
    # a dead path stayed plausible for ~15 minor versions. Deleted; see the note atop this file.
    # ⚠ Calling hid_poll here is what makes every polling consumer also a drain, so a caller parked in a
    # spin still services the ring even if the MSI-X on vector 0x51 is missed.
    hid_poll();
    # ⭐ THREAD-CONTEXT HALF OF THE HID DRAIN. hid_poll runs in ISR context too, where it may neither
    # touch the console (console_spin_lock has no cli and no owner check -> same-CPU deadlock) nor issue
    # a command that blocks on a Completion Event. It therefore records intent in flags; this call is
    # what acts on them. kb_has_key is reached only from ring 3 via a syscall, so it is safe here.
    # ⚠ It is placed AFTER hid_poll deliberately: a report drained a microsecond ago should flush its
    # one-shot on this same pass rather than waiting for the next poll.
    hid_service_deferred();
    # 1.53.7's dispositive "xhci: RX MSI LIVE (… vector 0x51)" proof was REMOVED at 1.53.14 once the
    # interrupt-driven USB-HID path was iron-validated on real AMD FCH — printed from this read loop,
    # it splattered onto the shell prompt. The keyboard responding IS the live signal now, and the
    # polled hid_poll() above still services keys if the MSI-X path ever regresses.
    if (kb_head != kb_tail) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

fn kb_read_scancode() {
    # input_lock: serialize the kb_tail RMW vs a producer (kb_isr/hid_kb_push) on
    # another CPU; cli-first excludes the same-CPU IRQ1 producer (see input_lock hdr).
    var f = kbd_irq_save();
    input_spin_lock();
    var sc = load8(&kb_buf + kb_tail);
    kb_tail = (kb_tail + 1) & 0xFF;
    input_spin_unlock();
    kbd_irq_restore(f);
    return sc;
}

# Enable / disable maskable interrupts (sti / cli). ⚠ THE NAME IS HISTORICAL — these are bare `sti` and
# `cli` with no port I/O and nothing keyboard-specific; `hid_kb_push` itself uses the save/restore pair.
# They are kept because the SYSCALL instruction masks IF on entry, so a syscall that must let an
# interrupt land has to re-enable it explicitly. ⭐ What they let land is now the **xHCI MSI-X on vector
# 0x51** (and the 100 Hz LAPIC tick that also drives `hid_poll`) — NOT IRQ1, which is deleted along with
# the rest of the PS/2 path. Arch-factored so core/syscall.cyr stays portable (aarch64 stubs these out).
fn kbd_irq_enable()  { asm { 0xFB; } return 0; }   # sti
fn kbd_irq_disable() { asm { 0xFA; } return 0; }   # cli

# === Scancode-to-ASCII (US QWERTY, Set 1) ===
# Full keyboard map with shift support.
# Lookup tables: 128 bytes each, indexed by scancode.

var kb_shift = 0;
var kb_caps = 0;
var kb_ctrl = 0;

# Unshifted scancode table (128 entries, 1 byte each)
var sc_normal[128];
# Shifted scancode table
var sc_shifted[128];

fn kb_init_tables() {
    # Zero both tables
    for (var i = 0; i < 128; i = i + 1) {
        store8(&sc_normal + i, 0);
        store8(&sc_shifted + i, 0);
    }
    # Escape (0x01)
    store8(&sc_normal + 1, 27);    store8(&sc_shifted + 1, 27);
    # Number row: 1-9, 0 (scancodes 0x02-0x0B)
    store8(&sc_normal + 2, 49);    store8(&sc_shifted + 2, 33);    # 1 !
    store8(&sc_normal + 3, 50);    store8(&sc_shifted + 3, 64);    # 2 @
    store8(&sc_normal + 4, 51);    store8(&sc_shifted + 4, 35);    # 3 #
    store8(&sc_normal + 5, 52);    store8(&sc_shifted + 5, 36);    # 4 $
    store8(&sc_normal + 6, 53);    store8(&sc_shifted + 6, 37);    # 5 %
    store8(&sc_normal + 7, 54);    store8(&sc_shifted + 7, 94);    # 6 ^
    store8(&sc_normal + 8, 55);    store8(&sc_shifted + 8, 38);    # 7 &
    store8(&sc_normal + 9, 56);    store8(&sc_shifted + 9, 42);    # 8 *
    store8(&sc_normal + 10, 57);   store8(&sc_shifted + 10, 40);   # 9 (
    store8(&sc_normal + 11, 48);   store8(&sc_shifted + 11, 41);   # 0 )
    # - = (0x0C-0x0D)
    store8(&sc_normal + 12, 45);   store8(&sc_shifted + 12, 95);   # - _
    store8(&sc_normal + 13, 61);   store8(&sc_shifted + 13, 43);   # = +
    # Backspace (0x0E), Tab (0x0F)
    store8(&sc_normal + 14, 8);    store8(&sc_shifted + 14, 8);    # backspace
    store8(&sc_normal + 15, 9);    store8(&sc_shifted + 15, 9);    # tab
    # QWERTY row (0x10-0x1B)
    store8(&sc_normal + 16, 113);  store8(&sc_shifted + 16, 81);   # q Q
    store8(&sc_normal + 17, 119);  store8(&sc_shifted + 17, 87);   # w W
    store8(&sc_normal + 18, 101);  store8(&sc_shifted + 18, 69);   # e E
    store8(&sc_normal + 19, 114);  store8(&sc_shifted + 19, 82);   # r R
    store8(&sc_normal + 20, 116);  store8(&sc_shifted + 20, 84);   # t T
    store8(&sc_normal + 21, 121);  store8(&sc_shifted + 21, 89);   # y Y
    store8(&sc_normal + 22, 117);  store8(&sc_shifted + 22, 85);   # u U
    store8(&sc_normal + 23, 105);  store8(&sc_shifted + 23, 73);   # i I
    store8(&sc_normal + 24, 111);  store8(&sc_shifted + 24, 79);   # o O
    store8(&sc_normal + 25, 112);  store8(&sc_shifted + 25, 80);   # p P
    store8(&sc_normal + 26, 91);   store8(&sc_shifted + 26, 123);  # [ {
    store8(&sc_normal + 27, 93);   store8(&sc_shifted + 27, 125);  # ] }
    # Enter (0x1C)
    store8(&sc_normal + 28, 10);   store8(&sc_shifted + 28, 10);   # enter
    # ASDF row (0x1E-0x28)
    store8(&sc_normal + 30, 97);   store8(&sc_shifted + 30, 65);   # a A
    store8(&sc_normal + 31, 115);  store8(&sc_shifted + 31, 83);   # s S
    store8(&sc_normal + 32, 100);  store8(&sc_shifted + 32, 68);   # d D
    store8(&sc_normal + 33, 102);  store8(&sc_shifted + 33, 70);   # f F
    store8(&sc_normal + 34, 103);  store8(&sc_shifted + 34, 71);   # g G
    store8(&sc_normal + 35, 104);  store8(&sc_shifted + 35, 72);   # h H
    store8(&sc_normal + 36, 106);  store8(&sc_shifted + 36, 74);   # j J
    store8(&sc_normal + 37, 107);  store8(&sc_shifted + 37, 75);   # k K
    store8(&sc_normal + 38, 108);  store8(&sc_shifted + 38, 76);   # l L
    store8(&sc_normal + 39, 59);   store8(&sc_shifted + 39, 58);   # ; :
    store8(&sc_normal + 40, 39);   store8(&sc_shifted + 40, 34);   # ' "
    # Backtick (0x29)
    store8(&sc_normal + 41, 96);   store8(&sc_shifted + 41, 126);  # ` ~
    # Backslash (0x2B)
    store8(&sc_normal + 43, 92);   store8(&sc_shifted + 43, 124);  # \ |
    # ZXCV row (0x2C-0x35)
    store8(&sc_normal + 44, 122);  store8(&sc_shifted + 44, 90);   # z Z
    store8(&sc_normal + 45, 120);  store8(&sc_shifted + 45, 88);   # x X
    store8(&sc_normal + 46, 99);   store8(&sc_shifted + 46, 67);   # c C
    store8(&sc_normal + 47, 118);  store8(&sc_shifted + 47, 86);   # v V
    store8(&sc_normal + 48, 98);   store8(&sc_shifted + 48, 66);   # b B
    store8(&sc_normal + 49, 110);  store8(&sc_shifted + 49, 78);   # n N
    store8(&sc_normal + 50, 109);  store8(&sc_shifted + 50, 77);   # m M
    store8(&sc_normal + 51, 44);   store8(&sc_shifted + 51, 60);   # , <
    store8(&sc_normal + 52, 46);   store8(&sc_shifted + 52, 62);   # . >
    store8(&sc_normal + 53, 47);   store8(&sc_shifted + 53, 63);   # / ?
    # Space (0x39)
    store8(&sc_normal + 57, 32);   store8(&sc_shifted + 57, 32);   # space
    return 0;
}

# Process a raw scancode: handle modifier keys, return ASCII or 0.
fn scancode_to_ascii(sc) {
    # Key release (bit 7 set)
    if (sc > 127) {
        var release = sc & 0x7F;
        # Left/right shift release
        if (release == 42) { kb_shift = 0; return 0; }
        if (release == 54) { kb_shift = 0; return 0; }
        # Left/right ctrl release
        if (release == 29) { kb_ctrl = 0; return 0; }
        return 0;
    }
    # Modifier key press
    if (sc == 42) { kb_shift = 1; return 0; }   # left shift
    if (sc == 54) { kb_shift = 1; return 0; }   # right shift
    if (sc == 29) { kb_ctrl = 1; return 0; }    # left ctrl
    if (sc == 58) {                              # caps lock toggle
        if (kb_caps == 0) { kb_caps = 1; }
        else { kb_caps = 0; }
        return 0;
    }

    # ⛔ 1.56.52 — THIS WAS `if (sc >= 128) { return 0; }` AND IT WAS VACUOUS. The release branch at the
    # top opens with `if (sc > 127)` and every path inside it returns, so control cannot reach here with
    # sc >= 128 and the check could never fire. It read as a bounds check while screening nothing.
    # ⚠ The index it FAILED to screen is a NEGATIVE sc: signed `sc > 127` is false, `sc >= 128` is
    # false, and `load8(&sc_normal + sc)` then reads BELOW the table. Cyrius has no unsigned types, so
    # every bound here is a signed compare — the same operand-wise reasoning the ramdisk and virtio
    # fixes in this cut needed. ⚠ SIX call sites, not two, and none is the PS/2 `inb` path an earlier draft
    # of this comment named — that was deleted with the i8042 (operator ruling 2026-08-08). They are
    # usb/hid.cyr, usb/hid_translate.cyr, core/devs.cyr, core/syscall.cyr (x2) and user/shell.cyr, and
    # every one sources its scancode from the USB HID stack as a 0..255 byte. So this is a LATENT hole,
    # not a live one — and closing it stops the safety depending on a property of six callers that
    # nothing enforces, for one compare in a path that already does four.
    if (sc < 0) { return 0; }
    if (sc >= 128) { return 0; }

    # Look up in table
    var ch = 0;
    if (kb_shift == 1) {
        ch = load8(&sc_shifted + sc);
    } else {
        ch = load8(&sc_normal + sc);
    }

    # Apply caps lock (only affects a-z / A-Z)
    if (kb_caps == 1) {
        if (ch >= 97) {
            if (ch <= 122) {
                # lowercase → uppercase
                ch = ch - 32;
            }
        } else {
            if (ch >= 65) {
                if (ch <= 90) {
                    # uppercase → lowercase (shift+caps = lower)
                    ch = ch + 32;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # Ctrl combinations: Ctrl+C = 3, Ctrl+D = 4, Ctrl+Z = 26, etc.
    if (kb_ctrl == 1) {
        if (ch >= 97) {
            if (ch <= 122) {
                return ch - 96;  # ctrl+a=1, ctrl+b=2, ... ctrl+z=26
            }
        }
    }

    return ch;
}
